  "content": {
    "id": "p5-q14-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "14",
    "prompt": "If $(a+b)^2+(b+c)^2+(c+d)^2=4(ab+bc+cd)$, prove that $a=b=c=d$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$a=b=c=d$.\n\nRearrangement gives $(a-b)^2+(b-c)^2+(c-d)^2=0$, so each square is zero.",
      "worked": "From the given equality,\n$$\\begin{align*}0&=(a+b)^2+(b+c)^2+(c+d)^2-4(ab+bc+cd)\\\\0&=a^2+2ab+b^2+b^2+2bc+c^2+c^2+2cd+d^2-4ab-4bc-4cd\\\\0&=a^2-2ab+b^2+b^2-2bc+c^2+c^2-2cd+d^2\\\\0&=(a-b)^2+(b-c)^2+(c-d)^2.\\end{align*}$$\nEach square is non-negative, so their sum is zero only when all three are zero. Thus $a-b=0$, $b-c=0$ and $c-d=0$, giving $a=b=c=d$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 49
  },
